---
import { Code } from 'astro:components'

import { examples } from '../examples.ts'
import Gallery from '../layouts/Gallery.astro'
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro'
import { componentLabel, mainMaxWidth } from '../siteMeta.ts'
import engineCode from '../browser/engine.ts?raw'
---

<Shell
  title="Build your own"
  componentLabel={componentLabel}
  examples={examples}
  mainMaxWidth={mainMaxWidth}
>
  <h1>Build your own genome browser</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    JBrowse ships a finished linear genome browser, and most people should use
    it. But underneath it is a set of parts, and if you are embedding genomic
    data in an app that already has its own design system, you may want the
    parts instead. These pages take the browser apart and put it back together
    one piece at a time.
  </p>

  <div class="doc">
    <h2>The two halves</h2>
    <p>
      A genome browser is an <strong>engine</strong> and some <strong
        >chrome</strong
      >. The engine resolves assemblies, chooses adapters, fetches and parses off
      the main thread, and keeps the coordinate state. The chrome is the search
      box, the ruler, the track labels, the loading spinner: everything around the
      data.
    </p>
    <p>
      The engine is the hard half and you should not rebuild it. One call gets
      you all of it, plus a view model that knows where it is in the genome and
      draws nothing:
    </p>
    <Code code={engineCode} lang="ts" />
    <p>
      From there every page in the sidebar adds exactly one thing. By the last
      one you have a browser with pan, zoom, three kinds of track, your own
      status overlays and your own ruler.
    </p>

    <h2>What you actually save</h2>
    <p>
      Measured on the eager (pre-first-paint) bundle, with React and MobX
      external: the chrome layer costs <strong>302 KB</strong> with JBrowse's
      own status components and <strong>140 KB</strong> with plain ones. The
      difference is almost entirely Material UI, and almost all of that is the
      loading overlay.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bundle size is the smaller half of the argument. The larger half is that
      no CSS-in-JS is injected into your page and nothing on screen has to be
      talked out of looking like a Material widget.
    </p>

    <h2>What you do not get rid of</h2>
    <p>
      Two honest limits, both of which these pages hit and neither of which is
      hidden in them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The palette.</strong> Stock feature and alignments displays read
      JBrowse theme tokens to colour their own <em>content</em> (reading frames,
      highlight boxes), so they still need <code>createJBrowseTheme</code>
      mounted through MUI's <code>ThemeProvider</code>. Swapping the overlays
      changes the status UI, not the palette. A wiggle track does not need it;
      the first three pages here have no theme at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The engine.</strong> Nothing on this site rebuilds adapters, the
      RPC layer, or the fetch lifecycle, and you should not either. What is on
      offer is control of the chrome, not a smaller JBrowse.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Gallery examples={examples} />
</Shell>
